We notice that the square on the diagonal connects the vertices $A$ and $B$. Therefore, the length of the diagonal is the hypotenuse of the right triangle $ABO$ for some point $O$. We can use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the length of the diagonal, which we can then subtract from the width of the field to find the difference in distance. See the diagram below.

[asy]
unitsize(0.2 cm);

pair A, B, O, X;

A = (0,0);
B = (300,0);
O = (150,0);
X = B;
draw(A--B);
draw(O--X);
draw(O--X,linetype("0 4"));

label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$O$", O, S);
label("$X$", X, SE);
[/asy]

The length of $AO$ is $150$ feet. By the Pythagorean Theorem, the length of the diagonal is $\sqrt{(150)^2+300^2} = \sqrt{30000} = \sqrt{100 \cdot 300} = 100\sqrt{300}$. The length of the field is $400$ feet. Therefore, Jim walks $400 - 100\sqrt{300} = 400 - 1000\sqrt{3}$ feet less than Martha. The answer is $\boxed{400 - 1000\sqrt{3}}$.

