\[\begin{aligned} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix} \times \mathbf{v} &= \begin{pmatrix} 90 \\ 30 \\ 30 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 15 \end{pmatrix} \times ( \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} ) \times \mathbf{c} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 15 \end{pmatrix} ( \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} ) \times \mathbf{c} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 15 \end{pmatrix} ( \mathbf{a} \times (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) ) \times \mathbf{c} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 15 \end{pmatrix} (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}) \times (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 15 \end{pmatrix} ( \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c} ) (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) = - \begin{pmatrix} 180 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} \end{aligned}\]Thus, $\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}$ has minimum magnitude. Note that we do not need to have $\mathbf{b}$ and $\mathbf{c}$ not orthogonal for $\mathbf{v}$ to be the smallest. ⋆ 
